-- title:
--   Acyclicity in degrees below the length of rs
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $rs$ a list of elements of $R$, and let $K : \mathbb{N} \to \mathrm{Type}$ be a family of $R$-modules. Assume that for every $i$ the list $rs$ is weakly regular on $K\,i$ in the sense of `RingTheory.Sequence.IsWeaklyRegular`, i.e. each entry of $rs$ acts as a non-zero-divisor on the quotient of $K\,i$ by the submodule generated by the action of the preceding entries. Let $\delta\,i : K\,i \to K\,(i+1)$ be $R$-linear maps with $\delta\,(i+1) \circ \delta\,i = 0$ for all $i$, so that $(K, \delta)$ is a cochain complex indexed by $\mathbb{N}$. Write $I =$ `Ideal.ofList rs` for the ideal generated by the entries of $rs$. Assume a torsion hypothesis in each degree: there is an $N$ such that every $a \in I^N$ annihilates every $z \in K\,0$ with $\delta\,0\,z = 0$; and for each $i$ there is an $N$ (depending on $i$) such that every $a \in I^N$ carries every $z \in K\,(i+1)$ with $\delta\,(i+1)\,z = 0$ into the range of $\delta\,i$. The conclusion is twofold: if the length of $rs$ is positive then $\delta\,0$ is injective on its kernel, i.e. $\delta\,0\,z = 0$ forces $z = 0$; and for each $i$ with $i + 1 <$ the length of $rs$, every $z \in K\,(i+1)$ with $\delta\,(i+1)\,z = 0$ lies in the range of $\delta\,i$.
--
--   This is the acyclicity lemma: a complex on which a sequence of length $d$ is weakly regular and whose cohomology is annihilated degreewise by a power of the ideal generated by the sequence has vanishing cohomology in all degrees below $d$. It is used in the computation of lengths and Euler characteristics, being cited by [`Module.toNat_length_ker_add_sum_neg_one_pow_toNat_length_eq_neg_one_pow_mul_toNat_length_quotient`](thm.html#Module.toNat_length_ker_add_sum_neg_one_pow_toNat_length_eq_neg_one_pow_mul_toNat_length_quotient).
